3.2.1 . KKT formulations of lower-level problem
For clarity, the dual variables and multipliers are defined explicitly for each and every constraint in the primal formulation of the lower-level problem. For the equality constraints, the dual variable
\lambda _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { P B } }
denotes that of the power balance equality, and
\lambda _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { S O C } }
represents the equality equation of the battery SoC dynamics.
For the inequality constraint, the dual variables
\mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { M T , l o } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { M T , u p } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { R a m p , u p } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { R a m p , d o w n } } .
are the multipliers for the MT constraints. Secondly,
\mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { e x , l o } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { e x , u p } }
are the dual variables for the exchange constraint between the ERs and lower level MGs. Lastly,
\mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { c h , l o } } , \quad \overset { \circ } { \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { c h , u p } } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { d i s , l o } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { d i s , u p } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { m u t } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { S O C , l o } } , \quad \mu _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { S O C , u p } }
are the dual multi-pliers for the primal battery constraints. Consequently, the Lagrangian equation is written as:
L _ { k } = J _ { k } + \sum _ { t \in \mathcal { T } } \lambda _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { P B } } \Big ( D _ { k , t } - P _ { \mathrm { M T } , k , t } - P _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { w i n d } } - P _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { s o l a r } } - P _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { d i s } } + P _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { c h } } - P _ { k , t } ^ { \mathrm { e x } } \Big )
